--------
Overview
--------

The migration library is responsible for checking whether or not the scheduler
has requested a migration, and if so, prepare and migrate threads between
architectures.  Currently, the scheduler cannot interrupt applications at
arbitrary points in execution to migrate threads.  Therefore, it has to set a
flag in userspace to request threads migrate.  When threads reach a known-good
migration point (e.g., one in which there is a valid state transformation
between ABI-specified state and a direct mapping in program execution
location), they check if the flag has been set.  If so, the migration library
initiates stack transformation to prepare for execution post-migration, and
invokes the OS's thread migration service via system call.

The migration library thus provides glue for scheduler policy call-outs, state
transformation (including a well-known location to bootstrap the runtime) and
thread migration.

For Unifico specifically, we add the following:

We add as part of the `lib/migration` timing infrastructure to mark the
time values (through `struct timeval`) for each phase of the migration.
There are two main functionalities added: a snapshot function and a
printing function. The snapshot is triggered each time a migration is
triggered. Then, it is expected that it will be triggered also from the
application itself, at the predefined points in the code where migration
is continued. This is because we need to measure the transfer time in
different time intervals.

An example for a migration x86->arm->x86 follows:

```text
x86
* (begin)
|
|
* (migrate)
------------arm
			* (continue in arm)
			|
			|
			|
			* (migrate)
x86----------
* (continue in x86)
|
|
|
* (end)
```

Essentially, we only want to take snapshots in the "stars". This way, the
difference between each consecutive snapshot will measure:
1. Execution on x86 until migration
2. Transfer time to arm
3. Execution on arm until migration
4. Transfer time to x86
5. Execution on x86 until the end

We also add  an additional userspace flag to control the triggering of
migration. Previously, `check_migrate` would only consult the `__indicator`
flag which is set externally by the tracer (the "scheduler" of the migration).
Now, it also consults a userspace flag that can only be set by the
application.  This is helpful because we want to precisely trigger migrations
at specific points in the code, so that our experiments are reproducible. This
cannot be achieved just by requesting migration through the scheduler, because
the timing cannot be controlled with respect to the execution of the
application itself.  Hence, the application at predefined points can set the
userspace flag, so that even if the scheduler requests a migration earlier, it
will only happen when the application allows it too.
